>> title: Birria de Res
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: beef, chiles, mexican, oven, braise
>> counters: Taquería
>> aka: birria, birria tacos, taco de birria, quesabirria, red taco
>> pairs-with: corn-tortillas, salsa-roja
>> servings: 8
>> time: 4 hr 30 min
>> slack: forgiving — three hours or four, the beef is shreddable at one and better at the other, and the consomé keeps three days; where it goes wrong is the skillet at the start, because a chile toasted to ash is bitter through the whole adobo and no straining takes it back out
>> step.6: pour over
>> step.7: braise 325°F 3 to 4 hr

Preheat the #oven{} to 325°F (160°C).

Toast @dried guajillo chiles{6}(stemmed and seeded), @dried ancho chiles{3}(stemmed and seeded) in a dry #skillet{}.

Blend @&(~1)toasted chiles{}, @white onion{1%large}(quartered), @garlic{5%cloves}, @cider vinegar{3%Tbs}, @ground cumin{1%tsp}, @Mexican oregano{1%tsp}, @cinnamon stick{1}, @whole cloves{4}, @bay leaves{2}, @black peppercorns{1%tsp}, @water{4%cup}(950 mL; hot).

Strain @&(~1)adobo{} through a #fine-mesh sieve{}.

Season @beef chuck{3%lb}(1.4 kg; cut in 3-in pieces), @beef short ribs{1%lb}(450 g), @kosher salt{1%Tbs} in a #Dutch oven{}.

Pour @&(~2)strained adobo{} over @&(~1)seasoned beef{}.

Braise @&(~1)pot{} covered at 325°F until shreddable, ~{3-4%hr}.
